// CHAPTER 2: Communication Skills and Graphical Drawing (Term 1)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// Lesson 2.1: Graphical Communication and Drawing Conventions
blockNum = 0;
const ch2_lesson1 = [
  t(1, "## Why Do We Use Drawings in Technology?\n\nIn Technology, **graphical communication** is the use of drawings to share design ideas clearly with others. A good drawing can explain a design better than words alone.\n\n### Types of Drawings in Technology\n\n| Drawing Type | Purpose | When Used |\n|-------------|---------|----------|\n| **Freehand sketch** | Quick 3D drawing to explore ideas | During the Design step |\n| **Working drawing** | Accurate technical drawing with dimensions | Before the Make step |\n| **Oblique drawing** | 3D drawing on a grid with depth at 45° | To show a product in 3D |\n| **Isometric drawing** | 3D drawing on a 30° grid | To show a product in 3D |\n\n### Purpose of Graphics\n\n- **Develop ideas** — quickly sketch different solutions\n- **Communicate ideas** — show others what you plan to make\n- **Record dimensions** — give exact measurements for construction\n- **Show hidden detail** — reveal parts that are hidden from view"),
  t(2, "## Line Types and Drawing Conventions\n\nTechnical drawings use different types of lines, each with a specific meaning.\n\n### Standard Line Types\n\n| Line Type | Appearance | Used For |\n|-----------|------------|----------|\n| **Continuous thick (dark)** | ━━━━━━ | Visible outlines and edges of the object |\n| **Continuous thin (feint)** | ─────── | Construction lines, dimension lines, projection lines |\n| **Dashed line** | - - - - - | Hidden detail — edges you cannot see |\n| **Chain line** | ─ · ─ · ─ | Centre lines and axes of symmetry |\n\n### Drawing Rules\n\n1. Use a **sharp pencil** — HB for outlines, 2H for construction lines\n2. Use a **ruler** for all straight lines\n3. **Dark lines** for visible edges (press harder)\n4. **Feint lines** for construction and dimensions (press lightly)\n5. **Dashed lines** for edges hidden behind the object\n\n### Dimensioning\n\n- All dimensions are in **millimetres (mm)**\n- Use thin lines with arrowheads to show dimensions\n- Write the measurement **above** the dimension line\n- Keep dimensions **outside** the object where possible\n- Never dimension to hidden lines"),
  q(3, 'Which type of line is used to show edges that are hidden behind an object?',
    ['Dashed line', 'Continuous thick line', 'Chain line', 'Continuous thin line'], 0,
    'Dashed lines (short, evenly spaced dashes) show hidden detail — edges and outlines that you cannot see because they are behind other parts of the object.'),
  t(4, "## Freehand Sketching\n\n**Freehand sketching** is drawing without instruments (no ruler, no compass). It is used in the Design step of IDMEC to quickly explore ideas.\n\n### Tips for Good Freehand Sketches\n\n1. Use **light pencil lines** — you can erase mistakes easily\n2. Draw the basic shape first, then add details\n3. Show the object from an **angle** (3D view) so you can see more than one face\n4. **Label** your sketch with:\n   - Materials (e.g. cardboard, wood, plastic)\n   - Dimensions (e.g. 150 mm long)\n   - Important features (e.g. hinge, slot, joint)\n5. Add **notes** explaining how the design works\n6. Draw at least **two different ideas** so you can compare them\n\n### Sketching 3D Objects\n\nTo make a flat drawing look three-dimensional:\n- Draw the **front face** first\n- Add **depth lines** going backwards at an angle\n- Connect the depth lines to form the back face\n- The depth lines should all go in the **same direction** and be the **same length**\n\n### Common Mistakes\n\n- Drawing too small — use most of the page\n- Not labelling — sketches without labels are not useful\n- Only drawing one idea — you must show at least two"),
  fb(5, 'All dimensions on technical drawings are measured in ___ (mm). When sketching, you should draw at least ___ different design ideas.',
    ['millimetres', 'two'],
    'South African technical drawings use millimetres (mm) as the standard unit. The CAPS curriculum requires at least two design ideas so you can compare them and choose the best one.'),
  t(6, "## Oblique Drawing\n\nAn **oblique drawing** is a method of drawing objects in 3D using squared paper (grid paper).\n\n### Rules for Oblique Drawing\n\n1. Draw the **front face** as a true shape (actual size and shape)\n2. Draw **depth lines** going backwards at **45°** to the horizontal\n3. Make the depth **half** the actual measurement (this prevents the drawing from looking stretched)\n4. Use squared paper ('quadrant paper') with lines at 45° to help\n\n### Step-by-Step: Drawing a Rectangular Prism (Block)\n\n1. Draw the front rectangle (e.g. 60 mm wide × 40 mm tall)\n2. From each corner of the front face, draw lines going up-right at 45°\n3. Make these lines **half** the actual depth (e.g. if depth is 40 mm, draw them 20 mm long)\n4. Connect the ends of the depth lines to form the back face\n5. Use **dark lines** for visible edges\n6. Use **dashed lines** for hidden edges (edges at the back that you cannot see)\n\n### Why Use Oblique Drawing?\n\n- It shows a 3D picture of the object\n- The front face is **true shape** — accurate size and proportions\n- It is easier than isometric drawing for beginners\n- It is required by the Grade 7 CAPS curriculum"),
  q(7, 'In an oblique drawing, at what angle are the depth lines drawn?',
    ['45 degrees', '90 degrees', '30 degrees', '60 degrees'], 0,
    'In oblique drawing, the depth lines go backwards at 45° to the horizontal. The depth is drawn at half the actual measurement to prevent the drawing from looking distorted.'),
  t(8, "## Working Drawings\n\nA **working drawing** is an accurate technical drawing that gives all the information needed to make the product.\n\n### What a Working Drawing Must Include\n\n1. **Accurate dimensions** in millimetres\n2. **Correct line types** (thick for outlines, thin for dimensions, dashed for hidden detail)\n3. **Scale** — if the product is too large or too small to draw full size\n4. **Title block** — name, date, scale, drawing number\n5. **Material notes** — what each part is made from\n\n### Scale\n\nIf an object is too large to draw at actual size, you **scale it down**:\n\n| Scale | Meaning |\n|-------|--------|\n| 1:1 | Full size (actual size) |\n| 1:2 | Half size (every 10 mm on the object = 5 mm on the drawing) |\n| 1:5 | One fifth size |\n| 2:1 | Double size (used for very small objects) |\n\n### South African Context\n\nArchitects and engineers in South Africa use working drawings every day:\n- **House plans** must be submitted to the local municipality before building\n- **Bridge designs** by SANRAL engineers use precise working drawings\n- **Furniture makers** in the Western Cape use working drawings to cut wood accurately"),
  fb(9, 'In oblique drawing, the depth is drawn at ___ the actual measurement. A scale of 1:2 means the drawing is ___ size.',
    ['half', 'half'],
    'Oblique drawings use half-depth to prevent distortion. A scale of 1:2 means every measurement on the drawing is half the actual size of the object.'),
  q(10, 'What information must be included in a title block on a working drawing?',
    ['Name, date, scale, and drawing number', 'Only the name', 'Only the dimensions', 'A picture of the product'], 0,
    'A title block provides essential information: who drew it (name), when (date), what scale was used, and the drawing number. This ensures anyone reading the drawing knows the context.'),
];

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// CHAPTER 3: Structures (Term 2)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// Lesson 3.1: Types of Structures and Forces
blockNum = 0;
const ch3_lesson1 = [
  t(1, "## What Is a Structure?\n\nA **structure** is anything that is designed to support a load or withstand forces. Structures keep their shape under force and serve a specific purpose.\n\n### Types of Structures\n\n| Type | Description | Examples |\n|------|------------|----------|\n| **Shell structure** | A hollow structure with a thin outer skin that carries the load | Egg, cool-drink can, helmet, igloo, tortoise shell |\n| **Frame structure** | Made of separate parts (members) joined together, with open spaces | Eskom pylons, roof trusses, scaffolding, jungle gym |\n| **Solid structure** | A heavy, solid mass of material | Brick wall, dam wall, concrete pillar |\n\n### Natural and Human-Made Structures\n\n| Natural Structures | Human-Made Structures |\n|-------------------|----------------------|\n| Egg (shell) | Cool-drink can (shell) |\n| Spider web (frame) | Eskom pylon (frame) |\n| Tree trunk (solid) | Brick wall (solid) |\n| Skull (shell) | Motorcycle helmet (shell) |\n| Honeycomb (frame) | Roof truss (frame) |\n\n### South African Structural Examples\n\n- **Shell:** The calabash-shaped FNB Stadium (Soccer City) in Johannesburg\n- **Frame:** The mine headgear at Crown Mines, gold mine shaft towers across Gauteng\n- **Solid:** The Gariep Dam wall on the Orange River — the largest dam in South Africa"),
  t(2, "## Forces Acting on Structures\n\nForces push, pull, twist, or cut materials. Every structure must be strong enough to resist the forces acting on it.\n\n### The Five Forces\n\n| Force | What It Does | Example |\n|-------|-------------|--------|\n| **Compression** | Pushes or squashes the material (makes it shorter) | Your weight pushing down on a chair leg |\n| **Tension** | Pulls or stretches the material (makes it longer) | Pulling a rope in a tug-of-war |\n| **Bending** | Pushes and pulls at the same time (top compressed, bottom stretched) | A wooden plank bending when you stand on it |\n| **Torsion** | Twists the material around its axis | Wringing water out of a wet cloth |\n| **Shear** | Two forces pushing in opposite directions, trying to cut | Scissors cutting paper |\n\n### Loads on Structures\n\n| Load Type | Description | Examples |\n|-----------|------------|----------|\n| **Dead load** (static) | The permanent weight of the structure itself | Weight of walls, roof, floor |\n| **Live load** (dynamic) | Changing forces that come and go | People, furniture, vehicles, wind, rain |"),
  q(3, 'Which force stretches a material by pulling it from both ends?',
    ['Tension', 'Compression', 'Torsion', 'Shear'], 0,
    'Tension is a pulling force that stretches a material, making it longer and thinner. Think of stretching a rubber band — that is tension.'),
  t(4, "## Stability — Keeping Structures Upright\n\nA structure is **stable** when it does not topple over. Stability depends on two factors:\n\n### 1. Centre of Gravity (Centre of Mass)\n\nThe **centre of gravity** is the point where all the weight of an object seems to act. It is the balance point.\n\n- Objects with a **low centre of gravity** are more stable\n- Objects with a **high centre of gravity** are less stable and topple more easily\n\n### 2. Base of Support\n\nThe **base of support** is the area between the contact points where the structure touches the ground.\n\n- A **wide base** makes a structure more stable\n- A **narrow base** makes a structure less stable\n\n### The Rule of Stability\n\nA structure will **topple** when the vertical line through its centre of gravity falls **outside** its base of support.\n\n### Ways to Make a Structure More Stable\n\n| Method | How It Works | Example |\n|--------|-------------|--------|\n| Widen the base | More area to catch the centre of gravity | Traffic cone has a wide base |\n| Lower the centre of gravity | Less likely to tip past the base edge | Placing heavy items on the bottom shelf |\n| Add weight to the base | Lowers the centre of gravity | Sandbags at the base of a temporary fence |\n| Use guy ropes or bracing | Stops the structure from leaning | Guy wires on a tent or aerial mast |"),
  fb(5, 'A structure topples when its centre of gravity falls outside its ___. To make a structure more stable, you can widen the ___ and lower the centre of gravity.',
    ['base', 'base'],
    'Stability depends on the centre of gravity staying within the base of support. A wide base and low centre of gravity make toppling less likely.'),
  t(6, "## Strengthening Structures\n\nEngineers use several methods to make structures stronger without adding too much weight.\n\n### Triangulation\n\nThe **triangle** is the strongest shape in structural engineering.\n\n- A **rectangle** can be pushed into a parallelogram — it deforms easily\n- A **triangle** is rigid — it cannot change shape without breaking a member\n- Adding a **diagonal brace** to a rectangle creates two triangles, making it rigid\n\n### Other Strengthening Methods\n\n| Method | How It Works | Example |\n|--------|-------------|--------|\n| **Triangulation** | Adding triangles to make the structure rigid | Roof trusses, Eskom pylons |\n| **Cross-bracing** | Diagonal members across a rectangle | Scaffolding, shelving |\n| **Folding / Corrugation** | Bending a flat sheet into waves increases stiffness | Corrugated iron roofing ('sinki'), cardboard boxes |\n| **Laminating** | Gluing thin layers together | Plywood, windscreen glass |\n| **Buttress** | A supporting wall built against the main wall | Church walls, retaining walls |\n| **Adding a rib or gusset** | Extra material at joints for reinforcement | Triangular gusset plates at joints in roof trusses |\n\n### Corrugation in South Africa\n\nCorrugated iron ('sinki') is the most common roofing material in South Africa. The wave shape makes the thin sheet of galvanised steel strong enough to span between roof trusses without sagging."),
  q(7, 'Why is the triangle considered the strongest shape for structures?',
    ['It cannot change shape without breaking one of its members', 'It uses the most material', 'It has the fewest corners', 'It is the easiest to build'], 0,
    'A triangle is rigid because its shape is completely determined by the length of its three sides. A rectangle can be pushed into a parallelogram, but a triangle cannot change shape without something breaking.'),
  t(8, "## Stiffening Materials\n\nSometimes the material itself needs to be made stiffer, not just the shape of the structure.\n\n### Methods of Stiffening\n\n| Method | Description | Example |\n|--------|------------|--------|\n| **Folding** | Bending a flat sheet at the edges | A folded sheet of paper stands upright; a flat sheet flops |\n| **Tubing** | Rolling a flat sheet into a tube | A rolled-up newspaper is stiffer than a flat one |\n| **Corrugating** | Adding waves to a flat sheet | Corrugated cardboard, corrugated iron roofing |\n| **Adding ribs** | Attaching thin strips along the surface | Ribs on a plastic chair give it stiffness |\n\n### Practical Activity: Stiffening Paper\n\n1. Take a flat sheet of A4 paper and stand it on its edge — it flops over\n2. **Fold** the paper in half — it stands a little better\n3. **Roll** the paper into a tube — it can now support a small load\n4. **Corrugate** the paper (fold it into zigzags) — it is now very stiff\n5. **Fold the edges** of the paper to create flanges — like an I-beam\n\n### What This Teaches You\n\nThe same material (paper) can be made much stiffer simply by changing its **shape**. Engineers use this principle to make thin, lightweight materials strong enough for buildings and vehicles."),
  fb(9, 'Corrugated iron roofing is also called ___ in South Africa. The same material can be made stiffer by changing its ___.',
    ['sinki', 'shape'],
    'Corrugated iron roofing is commonly called "sinki" in South African townships and rural areas. Folding, rolling, and corrugating are ways to change the shape of a material to increase its stiffness.'),
  q(10, 'Which of the following is NOT a method of making a structure more stable?',
    ['Making the base narrower', 'Widening the base', 'Lowering the centre of gravity', 'Adding weight to the bottom'], 0,
    'Making the base narrower decreases stability because the centre of gravity is more likely to fall outside the base. Widening the base, lowering the centre of gravity, and adding weight at the bottom all improve stability.'),
];

// Lesson 3.2: Structures — Design and the Impact of Technology
blockNum = 0;
const ch3_lesson2 = [
  t(1, "## Case Study: Cell Phone Towers in South Africa\n\nCell phone towers are found throughout South Africa — in cities, towns, and rural areas. They are a perfect example of frame structures in everyday life.\n\n### Why Study Cell Phone Towers?\n\nThe CAPS curriculum uses cell phone towers as a case study because they combine several Technology concepts:\n- **Frame structures** — the tower is a lattice (triangulated) frame\n- **Stability** — towers must withstand strong winds without toppling\n- **Materials** — galvanised steel is used for strength and corrosion resistance\n- **Impact on society** — mobile communication has transformed South African life\n- **Environmental impact** — towers affect landscapes and visual pollution\n\n### Structural Features of a Cell Phone Tower\n\n| Feature | Purpose |\n|---------|--------|\n| **Triangulated lattice frame** | Maximum strength with minimum weight |\n| **Wide base** | Stability — prevents toppling in wind |\n| **Galvanised steel** | Resists corrosion (rust) from rain and moisture |\n| **Concrete foundation** | Anchors the tower firmly in the ground |\n| **Guy wires** (on some towers) | Additional support against wind |\n| **Antennas at the top** | Transmit and receive cell phone signals |"),
  t(2, "## Investigating Existing Structures\n\nWhen you investigate an existing product or structure, you use specific criteria:\n\n### Evaluation Criteria\n\n| Criterion | Questions to Ask About a Structure |\n|-----------|-----------------------------------|\n| **Fitness for purpose** | Does it do what it was designed to do? |\n| **Strength and rigidity** | Can it withstand the forces acting on it without breaking or deforming? |\n| **Stability** | Will it stay upright? Is the base wide enough? Is the centre of gravity low? |\n| **Safety** | Could it harm anyone? Are there sharp edges? Could it fall? |\n| **Aesthetics** | Does it look good? Does it fit in with its surroundings? |\n| **Environmental impact** | Does it cause pollution? Can it be recycled? Does it cause visual pollution? |\n| **Cost** | Was it affordable to build? Is it expensive to maintain? |\n\n### Writing a Design Brief for a Structure\n\nFor the Grade 7 PAT (Practical Assessment Task), you must write a design brief for a cell phone tower. A good design brief includes:\n\n1. **What** you will design and make (a model cell phone tower)\n2. **Specifications** (must be at least 40 cm tall, must support an antenna, must use triangulation)\n3. **Constraints** (materials: rolled paper dowels, elephant grass, or cardboard; budget: R20; time: 3 weeks)"),
  q(3, 'Why are cell phone towers built as triangulated frame structures?',
    ['Triangulation provides maximum strength with minimum weight', 'Triangles are the cheapest shape', 'They look more attractive than solid towers', 'There is no specific reason'], 0,
    'Triangulated frame structures are strong (triangles are rigid) and lightweight (open spaces between members reduce material and wind resistance). This combination is ideal for tall towers.'),
  t(4, "## The Impact of Technology on the Environment\n\nTechnology has both **positive** and **negative** effects on the environment and society.\n\n### Positive Impacts\n\n| Area | Benefit | SA Example |\n|------|---------|------------|\n| **Communication** | Connects people across distances | Cell phones connect rural and urban SA |\n| **Health** | Better medical treatment, clean water | Water purification in villages |\n| **Education** | Access to information and learning | Tablets and internet in schools |\n| **Transport** | Faster, safer travel | Gautrain in Gauteng |\n| **Energy** | Electricity for homes and businesses | Solar panels in the Northern Cape |\n\n### Negative Impacts\n\n| Area | Problem | SA Example |\n|------|---------|------------|\n| **Air pollution** | Factories and vehicles release harmful gases | Power station emissions in Mpumalanga |\n| **Water pollution** | Industrial waste pollutes rivers | Acid mine drainage in Gauteng |\n| **Land pollution** | Waste in landfills, chemicals in soil | Plastic waste in rivers and oceans |\n| **Visual pollution** | Structures that spoil the landscape | Cell phone towers in scenic areas |\n| **Resource depletion** | Non-renewable resources are used up | Coal mining depleting reserves |"),
  fb(5, 'Cell phone towers use ___ steel to prevent rusting. Visual pollution is caused when structures spoil the natural ___.',
    ['galvanised', 'landscape'],
    'Galvanised steel has a zinc coating that protects it from rust. Visual pollution occurs when human-made structures (towers, billboards, mining dumps) negatively affect the appearance of the natural environment.'),
  t(6, "## Designing and Building a Model Cell Phone Tower\n\n### The PAT Scenario\n\nCell phone towers are everywhere in South Africa. Your task is to design and make a model cell phone tower that demonstrates your understanding of structures.\n\n### Specifications for the Model\n\n- Must be at least 40 cm tall\n- Must use **triangulation** for strength\n- Must have a **wide base** for stability\n- Must include **cross-bracing** between the legs\n- Must support a small model antenna at the top\n- Must be built from lightweight materials (rolled paper dowels, elephant grass, or cardboard strips)\n\n### Materials You Can Use\n\n| Material | Why It Is Suitable |\n|----------|-------------------|\n| **Rolled paper dowels** | Light, easy to cut, can be made rigid by rolling tightly |\n| **Elephant grass** | Natural, strong, available in many areas |\n| **Cardboard strips** | Cheap, easy to cut and join |\n| **Glue / tape** | For joining members |\n| **String** | For binding joints and adding guy wires |\n\n### Joining Methods\n\n| Method | How | Best For |\n|--------|-----|----------|\n| **Glue (PVA)** | Apply and press together | Permanent joints |\n| **Tape** | Wrap around the joint | Quick temporary joints |\n| **String binding** | Wrap string tightly around the joint | Strong, neat joints |\n| **Gusset plates** | Glue small triangles of card at joints | Reinforcing corners |"),
  q(7, 'Which material can be used for gusset plates when building a model tower?',
    ['Small triangles of cardboard glued at joints', 'Large sheets of metal', 'Concrete blocks', 'Rubber bands'], 0,
    'Gusset plates are small triangular pieces of material (in a model, cardboard) glued at joints to reinforce them. The triangular shape adds rigidity and prevents the joint from twisting.'),
  t(8, "## Evaluating Your Model Tower\n\nAfter building your cell phone tower model, you must evaluate it against the original specifications.\n\n### Evaluation Checklist\n\n| Question | Yes / No | Evidence |\n|----------|----------|----------|\n| Is it at least 40 cm tall? | | Measure with a ruler |\n| Does it use triangulation? | | Count the triangles visible |\n| Is the base wide enough for stability? | | Gently push the top — does it topple? |\n| Does it have cross-bracing? | | Check for diagonal members |\n| Can it support a model antenna? | | Place a small weight at the top |\n| Is it neat and well-finished? | | Visual inspection |\n\n### Suggesting Improvements\n\nAfter testing, think about what you would change:\n- **Strength:** Could you add more triangulation or gusset plates?\n- **Stability:** Could you make the base wider or add guy wires?\n- **Aesthetics:** Could you paint it or make the joints neater?\n- **Environmental impact:** Could you disguise it as a tree (as some real towers are)?\n\n### Real-World Connection\n\nIn South Africa, some cell phone towers are disguised as **trees** (palm trees or pine trees) to reduce visual pollution. This is a design solution that balances technological needs with environmental concerns."),
  fb(9, 'Triangulation works because the ___ is the strongest shape in structural engineering. Some cell phone towers in South Africa are disguised as ___ to reduce visual pollution.',
    ['triangle', 'trees'],
    'Triangles are rigid and cannot change shape without a member breaking. Disguising towers as trees is a creative solution to reduce visual pollution in scenic or residential areas.'),
  q(10, 'What is the purpose of cross-bracing in a frame structure?',
    ['Diagonal members create triangles that prevent the frame from deforming', 'To make the structure heavier', 'To block wind from passing through', 'To make the structure look better'], 0,
    'Cross-bracing adds diagonal members to rectangular frames, creating triangles. This prevents the frame from collapsing sideways (deforming into a parallelogram). It is a form of triangulation.'),
];

