// Lesson 4.1: Simple Electric Circuits
blockNum = 0;
const ch4_lesson1 = [
  t(1, "## Introduction to Electricity\n\n**Electricity** is the flow of tiny charged particles called **electrons** through a conductor (like a copper wire). We use electricity for lighting, heating, cooking, communication, and running machines.\n\n### What You Need for a Circuit\n\nFor electric current to flow, you need four things:\n\n1. An **energy source** — a cell or battery to push the electrons\n2. A **conductor** — a material that allows electrons to flow (usually copper wire)\n3. A **load** — a device that uses the electrical energy (lamp, buzzer, motor)\n4. A **complete (closed) pathway** — no gaps in the circuit\n\nIf any part of the circuit is broken (an **open circuit**), current cannot flow.\n\n### Conductors and Insulators\n\n| Type | Description | Examples |\n|------|------------|----------|\n| **Conductor** | Allows electric current to flow through it | Copper, aluminium, steel, water, gold |\n| **Insulator** | Does not allow electric current to flow | Plastic, rubber, wood, glass, air, cloth |\n\n### Why Copper Wire?\n\nCopper is used for electrical wiring because it:\n- Conducts electricity very well\n- Is **flexible** — it can be bent without breaking\n- Is relatively affordable\n- Does not corrode easily"),
  t(2, "## Circuit Components and Symbols\n\nIn Technology, you use standard symbols to draw circuit diagrams.\n\n| Component | Symbol Description | What It Does |\n|-----------|-------------------|-------------|\n| **Cell** | One long thin line (+) and one short thick line (−) | Provides electrical energy (1.5 V) |\n| **Battery** | Two or more cells joined together | Provides more voltage |\n| **Lamp (bulb)** | Circle with an X inside | Converts electricity to light and heat |\n| **Switch** | Gap with a moveable arm | Opens or closes the circuit (on/off) |\n| **Buzzer** | Half-circle shape | Converts electricity to sound |\n| **Motor** | Circle with M inside | Converts electricity to movement (rotation) |\n| **LED** | Triangle with a line, two small arrows | Emits light; only works one way round |\n| **Resistor** | Rectangle | Slows down the flow of current |\n\n### Drawing Circuit Diagrams\n\n1. Use a **ruler** for all straight lines (horizontal and vertical only)\n2. Use the **correct symbol** for each component\n3. Draw a **complete loop** — start at the battery, go through all components, and return to the battery\n4. **Label** components where needed\n5. Keep the diagram **neat and clear**"),
  q(3, 'What four things are needed for an electric circuit to work?',
    ['Energy source, conductor, load, and a complete pathway', 'Battery, wire, switch, and tape', 'Lamp, motor, buzzer, and LED', 'Voltage, current, resistance, and power'], 0,
    'A working circuit needs: (1) an energy source (battery), (2) a conductor (wire), (3) a load (lamp, buzzer, etc.), and (4) a complete pathway with no gaps.'),
  t(4, "## Building a Simple Circuit\n\n### Step-by-Step: Setting Up a Simple Circuit\n\n1. Connect a wire from the **positive terminal** (+) of the cell to one side of the switch\n2. Connect a wire from the other side of the switch to one terminal of the lamp\n3. Connect a wire from the other terminal of the lamp back to the **negative terminal** (−) of the cell\n4. Close the switch — the lamp should light up!\n5. Open the switch — the lamp goes out (circuit is broken)\n\n### The Switch\n\n- **Closed switch** = circuit complete = current flows = lamp ON\n- **Open switch** = circuit broken = no current flows = lamp OFF\n\n### Short Circuits — DANGER!\n\nA **short circuit** happens when current takes a shortcut back to the battery without passing through the load.\n\n- A very **large current** flows through the wire\n- The wire gets **very hot** and can melt or catch fire\n- The battery drains very quickly\n- **Never** connect a wire directly from the positive to the negative terminal!\n\n### Safety Rules\n\n| Rule | Reason |\n|------|--------|\n| Only use batteries (1.5 V to 9 V) in school | Mains electricity (230 V) can kill |\n| Never create a short circuit | Causes overheating and fire |\n| Check connections before switching on | Prevents damage |\n| Disconnect the battery before changing the circuit | Prevents shocks |"),
  fb(5, 'When a switch is ___, the circuit is complete and current flows. A short circuit is dangerous because a very large ___ flows, causing overheating.',
    ['closed', 'current'],
    'A closed switch completes the circuit, allowing current to flow. A short circuit has very low resistance, so a dangerously large current flows, causing wires to overheat and potentially start a fire.'),
  t(6, "## Input, Process, and Output in Circuits\n\nElectrical components can be classified by their role:\n\n| Category | Role | Examples |\n|----------|------|----------|\n| **Input** | Provides energy or control | Cell/battery, switch, sensor |\n| **Process/Control** | Controls how current flows | Resistor, variable resistor |\n| **Output** | Converts electrical energy to another useful form | Lamp (light), buzzer (sound), motor (movement), heater (heat) |\n\n### Systems Diagram for a Simple Circuit\n\n| Input | Process | Output |\n|-------|---------|--------|\n| Battery provides electrical energy | Switch controls on/off | Lamp produces light |\n\n### Connecting Cells\n\n**Cells in series** (end-to-end, positive to negative):\n- Voltages **add up**: three 1.5 V cells = 4.5 V\n- More voltage = brighter lamp\n\n**Cells in parallel** (positive to positive, negative to negative):\n- Voltage stays the **same** (1.5 V)\n- Battery **lasts longer** (cells share the work)\n\n### Practical Observation\n\n| Setup | Voltage | Lamp Brightness |\n|-------|---------|----------------|\n| 1 cell | 1.5 V | Dim |\n| 2 cells in series | 3.0 V | Medium |\n| 3 cells in series | 4.5 V | Bright |"),
  q(7, 'Three 1.5 V cells are connected in series. What is the total voltage?',
    ['4.5 V', '1.5 V', '3.0 V', '6.0 V'], 0,
    'Cells in series: voltages add up. 1.5 V + 1.5 V + 1.5 V = 4.5 V. The lamp will be brighter because more voltage pushes more current through it.'),
  t(8, "## Practical Activity: Drawing Circuit Diagrams\n\n### Circuit 1: Simple Lamp Circuit\n\nDraw a circuit with: one cell, one switch, one lamp.\n- This is the most basic circuit\n- The switch controls whether the lamp is on or off\n\n### Circuit 2: Buzzer and Lamp\n\nDraw a circuit with: two cells in series, one switch, one lamp, one buzzer (in series).\n- Both the lamp and buzzer share the same current\n- The switch controls both\n\n### Circuit 3: Motor Circuit\n\nDraw a circuit with: battery (3 cells), one switch, one motor.\n- The motor converts electrical energy into rotary motion\n- Used in fans, toys, and electric tools\n\n### Correct Symbols — Common Mistakes\n\n| Correct | Common Mistake |\n|---------|---------------|\n| Cell: long thin line (+) and short thick line (−) | Drawing both lines the same length |\n| Lamp: circle with X inside | Drawing just a circle |\n| Switch: gap with moveable arm | Drawing a complete line (no gap) |\n\n### South African Context\n\nMany rural schools in South Africa learn about circuits using simple materials:\n- Cells from torches\n- Copper wire from old electrical cables\n- Small torch bulbs\n- Homemade switches from paper clips and drawing pins"),
  fb(9, 'Cells connected in series produce a ___ total voltage. In a circuit diagram, the positive terminal of a cell is drawn as the ___ line.',
    ['higher', 'longer'],
    'Series connection adds voltages together (higher total). The positive terminal is represented by the longer, thinner line in the cell symbol.'),
  q(10, 'Which component converts electrical energy into rotary (circular) motion?',
    ['Motor', 'Lamp', 'Buzzer', 'Resistor'], 0,
    'A motor (shown as a circle with M inside) converts electrical energy into rotary motion. Lamps produce light, buzzers produce sound, and resistors reduce current flow.'),
];

// Lesson 4.2: Electromagnets and Simple Electrical Systems
blockNum = 0;
const ch4_lesson2 = [
  t(1, "## Magnetism Review\n\n### What Is Magnetism?\n\nA **magnet** is an object that attracts certain metals — specifically **iron**, **steel**, **cobalt**, and **nickel**. These metals are called **magnetic materials** (or ferrous metals).\n\n### Types of Magnets\n\n| Type | Description | Example |\n|------|------------|--------|\n| **Permanent magnet** | Always magnetic, does not need electricity | Bar magnet, horseshoe magnet, fridge magnet |\n| **Temporary magnet** | Only magnetic when near another magnet or when current flows | Paperclip stuck to a magnet, electromagnet |\n| **Electromagnet** | A magnet made by passing electric current through a coil of wire | Scrapyard crane, electric bell, MRI machine |\n\n### Magnetic and Non-Magnetic Materials\n\n| Magnetic (attracted to magnets) | Non-Magnetic (not attracted) |\n|-------------------------------|----------------------------|\n| Iron, steel, cobalt, nickel | Copper, aluminium, plastic, wood, glass, gold, silver |\n\n### Important Note\n\nNot all metals are magnetic! **Copper**, **aluminium**, **gold**, and **silver** are metals but they are NOT attracted to magnets. Only iron, steel, cobalt, and nickel are magnetic."),
  t(2, "## Electromagnets\n\nAn **electromagnet** is a magnet that only works when electric current flows through it. Turn off the current and the magnetism disappears.\n\n### How to Make a Simple Electromagnet\n\n1. Take an **iron nail** (this is the core)\n2. Wind **insulated copper wire** around the nail in a coil (many turns)\n3. Connect the ends of the wire to a **battery** (cell)\n4. The nail becomes a magnet — it can pick up paper clips!\n5. Disconnect the battery — the nail loses its magnetism\n\n### What Makes an Electromagnet Stronger?\n\n| Change | Effect |\n|--------|--------|\n| More turns of wire (more coils) | **Stronger** magnet |\n| More cells (higher voltage) | **Stronger** magnet (more current) |\n| Thicker iron core | **Stronger** magnet |\n| Use a 'soft' iron core (not steel) | Better — soft iron loses magnetism when current stops |\n\n### Why Use Soft Iron?\n\n- **Soft iron** magnetises and demagnetises quickly — good for an electromagnet that must switch on and off\n- **Steel** stays magnetised even after the current stops — this is a **permanent magnet**, not useful for switching\n\n### South African Applications\n\n- **Scrapyard cranes** in Johannesburg and Durban use powerful electromagnets to lift and sort scrap metal\n- **Electric bells** in schools use an electromagnet to strike the bell repeatedly\n- **MRI machines** in hospitals (like Groote Schuur Hospital in Cape Town) use superconducting electromagnets"),
  q(3, 'What must you do to make an electromagnet stronger?',
    ['Add more turns of wire around the core', 'Use a wooden core', 'Use fewer cells', 'Remove the iron nail'], 0,
    'More turns of wire create a stronger magnetic field. You can also increase the voltage (more cells), use a thicker iron core, or use soft iron. A wooden core would not work because wood is not magnetic.'),
  t(4, "## Practical Investigation: Electromagnet Strength\n\n### Aim\n\nTo investigate how the number of coils (turns of wire) affects the strength of an electromagnet.\n\n### Equipment\n\n- Iron nail, insulated copper wire, 2 × 1.5 V cells, paper clips, cell holder\n\n### Method\n\n1. Wind **10 turns** of wire around the nail\n2. Connect to the battery\n3. Count how many paper clips the electromagnet picks up\n4. Disconnect and record the result\n5. Repeat with **20 turns**, then **30 turns**, then **40 turns**\n\n### Results Table\n\n| Number of Turns | Paper Clips Picked Up |\n|----------------|----------------------|\n| 10 | 2 |\n| 20 | 5 |\n| 30 | 8 |\n| 40 | 12 |\n\n### Conclusion\n\nAs the number of turns increases, the electromagnet picks up more paper clips. This shows that **more turns = stronger electromagnet**.\n\n### Fair Testing\n\nTo make this a fair test, we keep everything the same except the number of turns:\n- Same nail (same core)\n- Same battery (same voltage)\n- Same type and size of paper clips\n- Same wire\n- Only the number of turns changes"),
  fb(5, 'An electromagnet uses an ___ nail as a core. For a fair test, you change only ___ variable while keeping everything else the same.',
    ['iron', 'one'],
    'The iron nail core concentrates the magnetic field. A fair test requires changing only one variable (the number of turns) while keeping all others constant (same nail, battery, wire, and paper clips).'),
  t(6, "## Sorting Metals with Electromagnets\n\n### The Problem\n\nA scrap-metal dealer needs to sort magnetic metals (iron, steel) from non-magnetic metals (copper, aluminium, brass) for recycling.\n\n### The Solution: Electromagnets\n\nAn electromagnet can be used to separate ferrous (iron-based) metals from non-ferrous metals:\n\n1. **Switch ON** the electromagnet above a pile of mixed metals\n2. **Ferrous metals** (iron, steel) are attracted and picked up\n3. **Non-ferrous metals** (copper, aluminium) stay on the ground\n4. Move the electromagnet over a different container\n5. **Switch OFF** — the ferrous metals drop\n6. Now you have two separate piles for recycling\n\n### Why This Matters in South Africa\n\n- South Africa has a large **recycling industry**\n- Scrap metal dealers create jobs in townships and industrial areas\n- Different metals have different **scrap values**:\n\n| Metal | Magnetic? | Approximate Scrap Value |\n|-------|-----------|------------------------|\n| Copper | No | High (R80–R120/kg) |\n| Aluminium | No | Medium (R15–R25/kg) |\n| Steel | Yes | Low (R2–R5/kg) |\n| Brass | No | High (R50–R80/kg) |\n\n### Ethical Consideration\n\nThe scrap metal industry provides income for many South Africans, but theft of copper cables and telephone wire is a serious crime that disrupts telecommunications and railway services."),
  q(7, 'Which of the following metals would NOT be picked up by an electromagnet?',
    ['Copper', 'Iron', 'Steel', 'Nickel'], 0,
    'Copper is a non-magnetic (non-ferrous) metal and would not be attracted to an electromagnet. Iron, steel, and nickel are all magnetic (ferrous) metals.'),
  t(8, "## Design a Crane with an Electromagnet\n\n### PAT Scenario\n\nDesign and build a model crane that uses an electromagnet to lift and sort metal objects.\n\n### Design Requirements\n\n| Specification | Detail |\n|--------------|--------|\n| Must have a working electromagnet | Iron nail + coil + battery |\n| Must use a simple mechanism | Lever, pulley, or crank to raise/lower the arm |\n| Must use a frame structure | Triangulated for strength |\n| Must have a switch | To turn the electromagnet on and off |\n| Must be able to pick up paper clips or small nails | Demonstrate magnetic sorting |\n\n### Systems Diagram for the Crane\n\n| Input | Process | Output |\n|-------|---------|--------|\n| Battery provides electricity; hand turns crank | Crank raises/lowers arm; electromagnet switches on/off | Arm moves; magnet picks up or drops metal objects |\n\n### Combining Skills\n\nThis project combines several Grade 7 Technology topics:\n- **Structures** — the crane body must be a stable frame structure\n- **Mechanisms** — a crank or pulley system raises and lowers the arm\n- **Electrical systems** — the electromagnet circuit with a switch\n- **Design process** — IDMEC from start to finish\n\n### Communication\n\nDraw a **circuit diagram** for the electromagnet showing:\n- Battery (cells in series)\n- Switch\n- Coil of wire around iron nail\n- Use correct symbols and a ruler"),
  fb(9, 'An electromagnet in a scrapyard crane picks up ___ metals (like iron and steel) but not non-ferrous metals like ___ and aluminium.',
    ['ferrous', 'copper'],
    'Ferrous metals (iron, steel) are magnetic and attracted to the electromagnet. Non-ferrous metals (copper, aluminium, brass) are not magnetic and stay behind, allowing separation for recycling.'),
  q(10, 'Why is soft iron used as the core of an electromagnet instead of steel?',
    ['Soft iron loses its magnetism quickly when the current is turned off', 'Steel is too expensive', 'Soft iron is a better conductor of electricity', 'Steel is not magnetic'], 0,
    'Soft iron is easily magnetised and demagnetised — it becomes magnetic when current flows and loses its magnetism when current stops. Steel would stay permanently magnetised, making it useless as a switchable electromagnet.'),
];

