// Lesson 5.1: Cranks, Pulleys, and Mechanical Advantage
blockNum = 0;
const ch5_lesson1 = [
  t(1, "## Simple Mechanisms Revisited\n\nIn Term 1, you learned about levers and hydraulic/pneumatic systems. Now we look at two more important mechanisms: **cranks** and **pulleys**.\n\n### Why Do We Need Mechanisms?\n\nMechanisms allow us to:\n- **Multiply force** — lift heavy objects with less effort\n- **Change direction** — pull down to lift up\n- **Change type of motion** — convert rotary to linear (or vice versa)\n\n### Types of Motion\n\n| Type | Description | Example |\n|------|------------|--------|\n| **Rotary motion** | Movement in a circle | Bicycle wheel, clock hands, drill bit |\n| **Linear motion** | Movement in a straight line, one direction | Arrow flying, car on a straight road |\n| **Reciprocating motion** | Back-and-forth in a straight line | Sewing machine needle, piston in engine |\n| **Oscillating motion** | Swinging back-and-forth around a point | Pendulum, playground swing |"),
  t(2, "## The Crank\n\nA **crank** is a mechanism that converts **rotary motion** (circular) into **reciprocating motion** (back-and-forth), or the other way around.\n\n### How a Crank Works\n\n- A handle is attached **off-centre** to a rotating shaft\n- As the shaft rotates (rotary motion), the attached part moves back and forth (reciprocating motion)\n- This is the basis of how car engines work — pistons move back and forth, turning a crankshaft\n\n### Examples of Cranks\n\n| Application | Input | Output |\n|------------|-------|--------|\n| **Hand drill** | Turning the handle (rotary) | Drill bit spins (rotary, but faster) |\n| **Old sewing machine** | Foot pedal rocks back and forth | Needle moves up and down |\n| **Car engine** | Piston moves up and down (reciprocating) | Crankshaft rotates (rotary) |\n| **Bicycle pedals** | Feet push pedals in circles (rotary) | Chain drives the wheel (rotary) |\n\n### The Crank as a Second-Class Lever\n\nA crank can be thought of as a **continuously rotating lever**. The distance from the centre of the shaft to the handle determines the mechanical advantage.\n\n- **Longer crank handle** = more force but slower rotation\n- **Shorter crank handle** = less force but faster rotation"),
  q(3, 'What type of motion does a crank convert rotary motion into?',
    ['Reciprocating motion', 'Oscillating motion', 'Linear motion', 'Random motion'], 0,
    'A crank converts rotary motion (circular) into reciprocating motion (back-and-forth in a straight line), or vice versa. The car engine is the classic example — pistons reciprocate, the crankshaft rotates.'),
  t(4, "## Pulleys\n\nA **pulley** is a wheel with a groove around its edge, through which a rope or cable runs. Pulleys are used to **lift heavy loads** and **change the direction of a force**.\n\n### Types of Pulley Systems\n\n| Type | Description | MA | Direction Change? |\n|------|------------|------|------------------|\n| **Single fixed pulley** | One pulley fixed above, rope goes over it | 1 (no force gain) | Yes — pull down to lift up |\n| **Single movable pulley** | One pulley attached to the load, moves with it | 2 (halves the effort) | No |\n| **Block and tackle** | Combination of fixed and movable pulleys | 2, 3, 4+ (depends on number of ropes supporting the load) | Yes |\n\n### How to Calculate Pulley MA\n\nCount the number of **rope sections** supporting the load.\n\n- 1 rope section = MA of 1 (no force advantage)\n- 2 rope sections = MA of 2 (half the effort needed)\n- 4 rope sections = MA of 4 (quarter the effort needed)\n\n### The Trade-Off (Again!)\n\nWith a pulley system that has MA = 4:\n- You only need to pull with **¼ of the load's weight**\n- But you must pull the rope **4 times as far** as the load moves up\n- You save force but use more rope (distance)\n\n### South African Examples\n\n- **Construction cranes** on building sites use pulley systems\n- **Mine headgear** uses large pulley wheels (sheave wheels) to raise and lower the cage\n- **Flagpoles** at schools use a single fixed pulley to raise and lower the flag"),
  fb(5, 'A single fixed pulley has a mechanical advantage of ___ because it only changes the direction of the force. A block and tackle with 4 rope sections has an MA of ___.',
    ['1', '4'],
    'A single fixed pulley does not multiply force (MA = 1) — it just lets you pull down instead of lifting up. A block and tackle with 4 rope sections supporting the load gives MA = 4 (you pull with ¼ the effort).'),
  t(6, "## Combining Mechanisms\n\nReal machines use **combinations of simple mechanisms** working together. Understanding how to combine mechanisms is key to Technology.\n\n### Examples of Combined Mechanisms\n\n| Machine | Mechanisms Used |\n|---------|----------------|\n| **Bicycle** | Levers (brake handles), cranks (pedals), pulleys (chain and sprockets) |\n| **Fishing reel** | Crank (handle), pulley (spool), lever (rod) |\n| **Car jack** | Screw mechanism, lever (handle) |\n| **Crane** | Pulleys, levers, gears, motors |\n\n### Mechanical Advantage Review\n\n$$\\text{MA} = \\frac{\\text{Load (output force)}}{\\text{Effort (input force)}}$$\n\n### Worked Example: Pulley\n\nA block and tackle system has 3 rope sections supporting the load. The load weighs 300 N.\n\n$$\\text{MA} = 3$$\n\n$$\\text{Effort} = \\frac{\\text{Load}}{\\text{MA}} = \\frac{300}{3} = 100 \\text{ N}$$\n\nYou need only **100 N** of effort to lift a **300 N** load.\n\nBut you must pull **3 times** as much rope as the load moves up.\n\n### Key Principle\n\nAll mechanisms follow the same rule: **you cannot get more energy out than you put in**. If you gain force, you lose distance. If you gain speed, you lose force. This is the conservation of energy."),
  q(7, 'A block and tackle with 4 rope sections is used to lift a 200 N load. What effort is needed?',
    ['50 N', '200 N', '800 N', '100 N'], 0,
    'MA = 4 (four rope sections). Effort = Load ÷ MA = 200 ÷ 4 = 50 N. You only need 50 N of effort, but you must pull 4 times as much rope.'),
  t(8, "## Strengthening Frame Structures — Revision\n\nAs you build your crane model in Term 3, you need strong frame structures. Let us revise the key strengthening methods.\n\n### Structural Strengthening Methods\n\n| Method | Principle | Application to Crane Model |\n|--------|----------|---------------------------|\n| **Triangulation** | Triangles are rigid shapes | Build the crane tower from triangular sections |\n| **Cross-bracing** | Diagonals prevent sideways collapse | Add diagonal members between the tower legs |\n| **Gusset plates** | Triangular reinforcement at joints | Glue small card triangles at each joint |\n| **Wide base** | Lowers centre of gravity relative to base | Make the base wider than the top |\n| **Counterweight** | Balances the load on the arm | Add weight to the back of the crane |\n\n### Testing Your Crane\n\nAfter building, test your crane by:\n1. Loading progressively heavier weights\n2. Checking that the structure does not bend or twist\n3. Checking that the base is stable (does not topple)\n4. Testing the electromagnet (does it pick up and release objects?)\n5. Testing the mechanism (does the crank/pulley raise and lower the arm?)\n\n### Evaluating Against Specifications\n\nYour crane must meet all the specifications from your design brief:\n- Does it lift objects? (mechanism works)\n- Does it use an electromagnet? (electrical system works)\n- Is it structurally sound? (frame with triangulation)\n- Can it sort magnetic from non-magnetic metals? (electromagnet switches on/off)"),
  fb(9, 'A counterweight on a crane balances the ___ on the arm, preventing the crane from toppling forward. All mechanisms follow the rule that you cannot get more ___ out than you put in.',
    ['load', 'energy'],
    'The counterweight balances the load to prevent the crane from tipping. Conservation of energy means input energy always equals output energy — any gain in force is offset by a loss in distance.'),
  q(10, 'Which of the following correctly describes the trade-off in all mechanisms?',
    ['If you gain force, you lose distance; if you gain speed, you lose force', 'You can gain both force and speed at the same time', 'Mechanisms create energy from nothing', 'There is no trade-off in pulleys'], 0,
    'All mechanisms follow the conservation of energy. You can trade force for distance (levers, pulleys) or speed for force (gears), but you can never get more energy out than you put in.'),
];

