// CHAPTER 6: Processing — Food and Textiles (Term 4)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// Lesson 6.1: Food Processing and Emergency Nutrition
blockNum = 0;
const ch6_lesson1 = [
  t(1, "## What Is Processing?\n\n**Processing** means changing raw materials into useful products through a series of steps. In Grade 7, you will learn about two types of processing:\n\n1. **Food processing** — turning raw ingredients into meals and preserved food\n2. **Textile processing** — investigating materials used for clothing and shelter\n\n### Why Do We Process Food?\n\n| Reason | Explanation | Example |\n|--------|------------|--------|\n| **Preservation** | Make food last longer without spoiling | Canning, drying, freezing |\n| **Safety** | Kill harmful bacteria | Pasteurising milk, cooking meat thoroughly |\n| **Convenience** | Make food easier to prepare | Pre-cut vegetables, instant porridge |\n| **Nutrition** | Add vitamins and minerals | Fortified bread and maize meal in SA |\n| **Taste** | Improve flavour and texture | Adding spices, baking bread |\n\n### South African Context: Food Fortification\n\nSince 2003, South African law requires that **bread flour** and **maize meal** be fortified with iron, zinc, vitamin A, and B-vitamins. This helps prevent malnutrition, especially in communities that rely on these staple foods."),
  t(2, "## Emergency Food: The Refugee Scenario\n\nThe CAPS curriculum uses an emergency food scenario to teach processing skills.\n\n### The Scenario\n\n> *Emergency situations like floods, fires, or political strife can force people from their homes, creating refugees. These people need nutritious food that can be prepared quickly in difficult conditions.*\n\n### What Refugees Need\n\n| Need | Explanation |\n|------|------------|\n| **High energy** | Displaced people are often exhausted from travelling |\n| **Nutritious** | Must contain proteins, carbohydrates, vitamins |\n| **Easy to prepare** | May have limited cooking facilities |\n| **Long shelf life** | Must not spoil quickly in hot conditions |\n| **Affordable** | Large numbers of people must be fed on a limited budget |\n\n### Investigating Emergency Food\n\n1. What types of food can be supplied to a refugee camp?\n2. What mix of people will be present? (Children, elderly, adults)\n3. What are their nutritional needs?\n4. What cooking facilities are available?\n5. What food will be available under emergency conditions?\n\n### Nutritional Requirements\n\n| Nutrient | Function | Foods |\n|----------|----------|-------|\n| **Carbohydrates** | Energy | Maize meal (pap), bread, rice, potatoes |\n| **Proteins** | Growth and repair | Beans, lentils, eggs, tinned fish, soya |\n| **Fats** | Energy and warmth | Oil, peanut butter, margarine |\n| **Vitamins** | Body functions | Fruit, vegetables |\n| **Minerals** | Strong bones and teeth | Milk, dark green vegetables |"),
  q(3, 'Why does South African law require bread flour and maize meal to be fortified?',
    ['To prevent malnutrition by adding essential vitamins and minerals', 'To make the food taste better', 'To make it last longer', 'To make it cheaper'], 0,
    'Food fortification adds iron, zinc, vitamin A, and B-vitamins to staple foods. This helps prevent nutritional deficiencies, especially in communities that rely heavily on bread and maize meal.'),
  t(4, "## Design Brief: Emergency Meal\n\n### Your Task\n\nDesign a nutritious meal that could be served to 100 refugees in a camp. The meal must be:\n- **Nutritious** — contain at least carbohydrates, protein, and vegetables\n- **Affordable** — use ingredients that are cheap and widely available in South Africa\n- **Easy to prepare** — require minimal cooking equipment\n- **Tasty** — people must want to eat it\n\n### Example Meal Ideas\n\n| Meal | Ingredients | Nutrients Provided |\n|------|-----------|-------------------|\n| **Pap and bean stew** | Maize meal, dried beans, tomatoes, onion, oil | Carbs, protein, vitamins |\n| **Samp and beans** | Samp (crushed maize), sugar beans, salt | Carbs, protein |\n| **Vegetable soup with bread** | Potatoes, carrots, cabbage, bread | Carbs, vitamins, fibre |\n| **Lentil curry with rice** | Lentils, rice, onion, curry powder, oil | Carbs, protein |\n\n### Writing Specifications for the Meal\n\n1. Must feed 100 people\n2. Must provide at least 2 000 kJ per person\n3. Must include protein, carbohydrates, and at least one vegetable\n4. Must cost no more than R5 per person\n5. Must be prepared using only a fire and large pots\n\n### The Making Process\n\n1. Write down the sequence of steps (recipe)\n2. List all ingredients and quantities\n3. Prepare one serving as a test sample\n4. Evaluate: taste, texture, nutritional value, cost"),
  fb(5, 'A nutritious emergency meal should contain at least carbohydrates, ___, and vegetables. South African maize meal is also called ___.',
    ['protein', 'pap'],
    'A balanced meal needs energy (carbohydrates), building blocks (protein), and micronutrients (from vegetables). Pap (maize meal porridge) is the most common staple food in South Africa.'),
  t(6, "## Making and Evaluating Food Products\n\n### The Manufacturing Process\n\nWhen making food products, you follow a sequence of steps:\n\n1. **Gather ingredients** — measure quantities carefully\n2. **Prepare ingredients** — wash, peel, chop\n3. **Cook** — following the correct method and timing\n4. **Serve** — present the food attractively\n5. **Evaluate** — taste, texture, appearance, nutritional value\n\n### Evaluating Food Products\n\n| Criterion | Questions |\n|-----------|----------|\n| **Taste** | Is it pleasant to eat? Is it too bland or too spicy? |\n| **Texture** | Is it the right consistency? Not too watery or too thick? |\n| **Appearance** | Does it look appetising? Is the colour appealing? |\n| **Nutritional value** | Does it provide the nutrients needed? |\n| **Cost** | Is it affordable for the target group? |\n| **Shelf life** | How long can it be stored? |\n\n### Food Safety and Hygiene\n\n| Rule | Reason |\n|------|--------|\n| Wash hands before handling food | Prevents bacteria from spreading |\n| Cook meat and eggs thoroughly | Kills harmful bacteria (salmonella, E. coli) |\n| Keep hot food hot and cold food cold | Bacteria multiply in warm conditions |\n| Use clean utensils and surfaces | Prevents cross-contamination |\n| Check expiry dates | Expired food may contain harmful bacteria |"),
  q(7, 'Which traditional South African dish combines a carbohydrate and protein source in one meal?',
    ['Samp and beans', 'White bread only', 'Tea with sugar', 'Plain rice'], 0,
    'Samp and beans combines samp (crushed maize — carbohydrate) with sugar beans (protein). This traditional dish provides a good balance of energy and building nutrients.'),
  t(8, "## Investigating Textiles and Clothing\n\n### Why Study Textiles?\n\nTextiles (fabrics) are materials used for clothing, shelter, and other products. Understanding textile properties helps you choose the right material for a purpose.\n\n### Properties of Textiles\n\n| Property | Definition | Test |\n|----------|-----------|------|\n| **Waterproof** | Does not let water pass through | Pour water on the fabric — does it soak through? |\n| **Absorbent** | Soaks up water | Dip in water — how much does it absorb? |\n| **Durable** | Lasts a long time with use | Rub with sandpaper — how quickly does it wear? |\n| **Flammable** | Burns easily | Hold near a flame (with adult supervision) |\n| **Breathable** | Allows air to pass through | Blow through the fabric — can air get through? |\n| **Insulating** | Keeps heat in | Wrap around a warm bottle — measure temperature loss |\n\n### Natural vs Synthetic Textiles\n\n| Natural | Synthetic |\n|---------|----------|\n| Cotton, wool, silk, linen | Nylon, polyester, acrylic |\n| Comes from plants or animals | Made from chemicals (petroleum) |\n| Usually breathable | Often waterproof |\n| Biodegradable | Not biodegradable |\n\n### South African Context: Emergency Clothing\n\nPeople in emergency situations need clothing that is:\n- **Warm** (insulating) — for cold nights\n- **Waterproof** — for rain protection\n- **Durable** — to withstand rough conditions\n- **Affordable** — available in large quantities"),
  fb(9, 'Natural textiles like cotton come from ___ or animals, while synthetic textiles like polyester are made from ___.',
    ['plants', 'chemicals'],
    'Cotton comes from the cotton plant; wool comes from sheep. Synthetic textiles are manufactured from chemicals derived from petroleum (crude oil). Each type has advantages and disadvantages.'),
  q(10, 'Which textile property is most important for clothing worn in rainy conditions?',
    ['Waterproof', 'Flammable', 'Absorbent', 'Transparent'], 0,
    'Waterproof fabric does not let water pass through, keeping the wearer dry in rain. Absorbent fabric would be the worst choice as it soaks up water. Breathable waterproof fabrics (like Gore-Tex) are ideal.'),
];

