// CHAPTER 7: Impact of Technology and Indigenous Knowledge (Term 4)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// Lesson 7.1: Impact of Technology, Indigenous Building, and Emergency Shelters
blockNum = 0;
const ch7_lesson1 = [
  t(1, "## The Impact of Technology on Society and the Environment\n\nEvery technological product or system has an impact — some positive, some negative. As a Technology student, you must evaluate these impacts critically.\n\n### Positive Impacts of Technology\n\n| Area | Benefit | South African Example |\n|------|---------|----------------------|\n| **Health** | Better medicine, cleaner water | Water purification in rural KwaZulu-Natal |\n| **Communication** | Instant connection over long distances | Cell phones connecting remote areas |\n| **Transport** | Faster, safer travel | N1 and N2 highways, Gautrain |\n| **Agriculture** | More food production | Irrigation systems in the Western Cape |\n| **Energy** | Electricity for homes and schools | Eskom power grid, solar panels |\n\n### Negative Impacts of Technology\n\n| Area | Problem | South African Example |\n|------|---------|----------------------|\n| **Pollution** | Air, water, and land contamination | Factory emissions in Mpumalanga |\n| **Resource depletion** | Using up non-renewable resources | Coal mining |\n| **Job losses** | Machines replacing human workers | Automated mining equipment |\n| **Waste** | Electronic waste, plastic pollution | Plastic in rivers and on beaches |\n| **Health risks** | Harmful chemicals, noise, radiation | Asbestos-related diseases in old mining areas |"),
  t(2, "## Indigenous Technologies in South Africa\n\nSouth Africa has rich **indigenous knowledge systems (IKS)** — technologies and practices developed by local communities over centuries.\n\n### Examples of Indigenous Technology\n\n| Technology | Description | Modern Equivalent |\n|-----------|------------|-------------------|\n| **Rondavel** | Round house with mud walls and thatched grass roof (shell/solid structure) | Conventional house |\n| **Clay grain storage** | Raised clay pots keep grain dry and pest-free | Metal silos |\n| **Iron smelting** | Clay furnaces using charcoal to extract iron from ore — practised in SA for 1 800+ years | Modern blast furnaces |\n| **Beadwork** | Intricate patterns using tiny glass beads — communicates messages through colour | Decorative textiles |\n| **Fish traps** | Woven baskets placed in rivers to catch fish | Commercial fishing |\n| **Herbal medicine** | Traditional healers (sangomas/inyangas) using indigenous plants | Pharmaceutical drugs |\n\n### Indigenous Building Techniques\n\nIndigenous communities in South Africa used locally available materials:\n\n| Material | Where Found | Used For |\n|----------|------------|----------|\n| **Mud/clay** | Everywhere | Walls (cob construction) |\n| **Thatching grass** | Grasslands of KZN, Free State | Roofing |\n| **Wattle (branches)** | Forests and bushveld | Frame for mud walls |\n| **Cow dung** | Cattle-farming areas | Floor coating (smooth, hard, insect-repelling) |\n| **Stone** | Mountainous areas | Walls (dry stone walling) |\n\n### Why Study Indigenous Technology?\n\n- Uses **locally available**, renewable materials\n- Is **environmentally friendly** — low pollution, biodegradable\n- Is **culturally appropriate** — reflects community values\n- Offers **sustainable solutions** that complement modern technology"),
  q(3, 'Which of the following is an example of indigenous technology in South Africa?',
    ['Iron smelting using clay furnaces', 'Building with reinforced concrete', 'Manufacturing plastic products', 'Using solar panels'], 0,
    'Iron smelting using clay furnaces dates back over 1 800 years in South Africa. Communities in Limpopo, KZN, and Mpumalanga extracted iron from ore using locally built clay furnaces and charcoal.'),
  t(4, "## Building Materials and Their Properties\n\nChoosing the right material is essential when designing any product or structure.\n\n### Common Building Materials\n\n| Material | Properties | Advantages | Disadvantages |\n|----------|-----------|-----------|---------------|\n| **Wood** | Light, strong, easy to cut, insulating | Renewable, easy to work with | Burns, rots, attacked by insects |\n| **Brick** | Hard, fireproof, insulating | Durable, good compression strength | Heavy, brittle, needs mortar |\n| **Concrete** | Very strong in compression, mouldable | Cheap, long-lasting | Weak in tension, heavy, cracks |\n| **Steel** | Very strong, flexible | Strong in tension and compression | Corrodes (rusts), expensive |\n| **Plastic** | Light, waterproof, mouldable | Cheap, does not corrode | Burns, not biodegradable, melts in heat |\n| **Glass** | Transparent, hard, waterproof | Lets light in, easy to clean | Brittle, breaks easily |\n\n### Material Properties Vocabulary\n\n| Property | Meaning |\n|----------|--------|\n| **Strong** | Resists forces without breaking |\n| **Hard** | Resists scratching or denting |\n| **Flexible** | Can bend without breaking |\n| **Brittle** | Breaks suddenly without bending |\n| **Durable** | Lasts a long time |\n| **Waterproof** | Does not let water through |\n| **Insulating** | Does not conduct heat or electricity well |\n| **Corrosion resistant** | Does not rust or deteriorate chemically |"),
  fb(5, 'Concrete is strong in compression but weak in ___. Indigenous builders in South Africa used cow dung to coat floors because it creates a smooth, hard, ___-repelling surface.',
    ['tension', 'insect'],
    'Concrete handles compression well but cracks under tension (pulling forces). Traditional cow dung floors are smooth, dry hard, and the chemical properties repel insects — a clever indigenous solution.'),
  t(6, "## Emergency Shelters\n\nThe CAPS curriculum requires you to investigate and design emergency shelters for disaster victims.\n\n### The Scenario\n\n> *Natural disasters like floods, fires, or earthquakes can make people homeless. Emergency shelters must be erected quickly to protect families from the weather.*\n\n### Requirements for an Emergency Shelter\n\n| Requirement | Explanation |\n|------------|------------|\n| **Quick to erect** | Must be set up in hours, not days |\n| **Waterproof** | Must keep out rain |\n| **Sturdy** | Must withstand wind and weather |\n| **Portable** | Must be easy to transport to the disaster site |\n| **Affordable** | Must be low-cost to produce in large numbers |\n| **Houses a family** | Must fit at least 6 people |\n| **Safe** | Must not collapse on occupants |\n\n### Materials for Emergency Shelters\n\n| Material | Advantages | Disadvantages |\n|----------|-----------|---------------|\n| **Tarpaulin (plastic sheet)** | Waterproof, cheap, light | Tears easily, not insulating |\n| **Canvas** | Strong, breathable, waterproof | Heavier, more expensive |\n| **Corrugated iron** | Strong, waterproof | Heavy, gets very hot/cold |\n| **Bamboo / wooden poles** | Strong frame, lightweight | Availability varies |\n| **PVC pipes** | Light, easy to join, reusable | Not very rigid |\n\n### Design Considerations\n\n- Use a **frame structure** (tent-like) or a **shell structure** (dome) for quick assembly\n- Ensure the base is raised or drained to prevent flooding\n- Include ventilation to prevent condensation\n- Consider health and safety issues (fire risk, privacy, sanitation)"),
  q(7, 'Which of the following is the most important requirement for an emergency shelter?',
    ['Quick to erect and waterproof', 'Beautiful appearance', 'Permanent construction', 'Made from expensive materials'], 0,
    'Emergency shelters must be set up quickly (people need immediate protection) and must be waterproof (rain is a common problem after floods and storms). Appearance and permanence are less important than speed and weather protection.'),
  t(8, "## Design Brief: Emergency Shelter\n\n### Your Task\n\nDesign and make a model of an emergency shelter for a family of six people displaced by a flood in South Africa.\n\n### Specifications\n\n1. Must shelter at least 6 people (scale model)\n2. Must be **waterproof** — able to keep out rain\n3. Must be **sturdy** — not blow over in wind\n4. Must be **easy to erect** — assembly within 30 minutes (real scale)\n5. Must be **portable** — can be transported in a vehicle\n6. Must use a **frame structure** with a waterproof covering\n\n### Constraints\n\n- Materials: PVC pipes, bamboo sticks, tarpaulin, string, tape\n- Budget: R40 for model materials\n- Time: 2 weeks\n- Maximum model size: 30 cm × 30 cm × 25 cm\n\n### Applying IDMEC\n\n| Step | What You Do |\n|------|------------|\n| **Investigate** | Research emergency shelters used by organisations like Gift of the Givers |\n| **Design** | Write the design brief, list specifications, sketch 2 ideas |\n| **Make** | Build the model using your chosen materials |\n| **Evaluate** | Test: is it waterproof? Is it stable? Does it fit 6 toy figures? |\n| **Communicate** | Present your model with drawings and explanation |\n\n### South African Connection\n\nOrganisations like **Gift of the Givers** regularly provide emergency shelters after natural disasters in South Africa. In 2022, devastating floods in KwaZulu-Natal left thousands homeless, requiring emergency shelter solutions."),
  fb(9, 'An emergency shelter must be ___ to prevent rain from entering and ___ to prevent it from blowing over in wind.',
    ['waterproof', 'sturdy'],
    'Waterproofing keeps occupants dry and healthy. Sturdiness ensures the shelter withstands wind loads. These are the two most critical functional requirements after speed of assembly.'),
  q(10, 'Which South African organisation is well-known for providing emergency relief, including shelters, after natural disasters?',
    ['Gift of the Givers', 'Eskom', 'SANRAL', 'Sasol'], 0,
    'Gift of the Givers is South Africa\'s largest disaster response organisation. They provide emergency food, water, shelter, and medical care after floods, fires, and other disasters.'),
];

