// CHAPTER 8: Revision (Term 4)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

blockNum = 0;
const ch8_lesson1 = [
  t(1, "## Grade 7 Technology — Comprehensive Revision\n\n### The Design Process (IDMEC)\n\n| Step | Key Activities |\n|------|---------------|\n| **Investigate** | Research the problem, analyse existing products, identify user needs |\n| **Design** | Write design brief (specifications + constraints), sketch 2+ ideas, choose the best |\n| **Make** | Flow chart of steps, list tools/materials, build safely |\n| **Evaluate** | Test against specifications, identify strengths/weaknesses, suggest improvements |\n| **Communicate** | Present with sketches, drawings, model, explanation |\n\n### Mechanisms\n\n**Levers:**\n- Class 1: Fulcrum between Effort and Load (seesaw, scissors)\n- Class 2: Load between Fulcrum and Effort (wheelbarrow, nutcracker)\n- Class 3: Effort between Fulcrum and Load (tweezers, fishing rod)\n\n**Mechanical Advantage:** MA = Load ÷ Effort\n- MA > 1: force is multiplied\n- MA < 1: speed/distance is multiplied\n- Trade-off: gain in force = loss in distance\n\n**Hydraulic vs Pneumatic:**\n- Hydraulic uses liquid (incompressible) — smooth, precise force\n- Pneumatic uses gas (compressible) — fast, clean, slightly springy"),
  t(2, "### Structures\n\n**Types of structures:**\n- **Shell** — thin outer skin carries load (egg, helmet, can)\n- **Frame** — skeleton of members with open spaces (pylon, roof truss)\n- **Solid** — heavy mass of material (dam wall, brick wall)\n\n**Forces:** Compression (push), Tension (pull), Bending, Torsion (twist), Shear (cut)\n\n**Loads:** Dead load (permanent weight), Live load (changing forces)\n\n**Stability:**\n- Wide base + low centre of gravity = more stable\n- Structure topples when centre of gravity falls outside base\n\n**Strengthening methods:** Triangulation, cross-bracing, corrugation, folding, laminating, gusset plates, buttresses\n\n**Triangle** = strongest shape (cannot change shape without breaking a member)\n\n### Graphical Communication\n\n**Line types:**\n- Continuous thick: visible outlines\n- Continuous thin: dimensions, construction\n- Dashed: hidden detail\n- Chain: centre lines\n\n**Oblique drawing:** Front face true shape, depth at 45°, depth drawn at half actual size\n\n**Dimensioning:** All in millimetres (mm), thin lines with arrowheads"),
  q(3, 'What is the mechanical advantage of a lever that lifts a 600 N load with an effort of 200 N?',
    ['3', '6', '200', '800'], 0,
    'MA = Load ÷ Effort = 600 ÷ 200 = 3. The lever multiplies your force 3 times.'),
  t(4, "### Electrical Systems\n\n**Circuit requirements:** Energy source + conductor + load + complete pathway\n\n**Components and symbols:** Cell, battery, lamp, switch, buzzer, motor, LED, resistor\n\n**Cells in series:** Voltages add up (3 × 1.5 V = 4.5 V)\n**Cells in parallel:** Same voltage, longer battery life\n\n**Electromagnets:**\n- Made from iron nail + coil of copper wire + battery\n- Stronger with: more turns, more cells, thicker iron core\n- Soft iron core (loses magnetism when current stops)\n- Used in scrapyard cranes to sort ferrous from non-ferrous metals\n\n**Magnetic metals:** Iron, steel, cobalt, nickel\n**Non-magnetic metals:** Copper, aluminium, gold, silver, brass\n\n### Mechanisms (continued)\n\n**Crank:** Converts rotary motion ↔ reciprocating motion\n\n**Pulleys:**\n- Single fixed pulley: MA = 1 (changes direction only)\n- Movable pulley: MA = 2\n- Block and tackle: MA = number of rope sections supporting the load\n\n**Trade-off in all mechanisms:** Gain force → lose distance. Gain speed → lose force."),
  fb(5, 'An electromagnet is made from a coil of copper wire wound around an ___ nail. Magnetic metals include iron, steel, cobalt, and ___.',
    ['iron', 'nickel'],
    'The iron nail acts as the core of the electromagnet. Iron, steel, cobalt, and nickel are the four magnetic (ferrous) metals that are attracted to magnets.'),
  t(6, "### Processing and Impact of Technology\n\n**Food processing reasons:** Preservation, safety, convenience, nutrition, taste\n\n**South African food fortification:** Bread flour and maize meal fortified with iron, zinc, vitamin A, B-vitamins since 2003\n\n**Emergency food requirements:** Nutritious, affordable, easy to prepare, long shelf life\n\n**Textiles:**\n- Natural: cotton, wool, silk (from plants/animals, biodegradable)\n- Synthetic: nylon, polyester (from chemicals, not biodegradable)\n- Key properties: waterproof, absorbent, durable, flammable, breathable, insulating\n\n**Indigenous technologies:**\n- Rondavels (round mud houses with thatch roofs)\n- Clay grain storage (raised pots, pest-free)\n- Iron smelting (1 800+ years ago, clay furnaces, charcoal)\n- Beadwork (communicates through colour patterns)\n- Cow dung floors (smooth, hard, insect-repelling)\n\n**Impact of technology:**\n- Positive: communication, health, transport, energy, agriculture\n- Negative: pollution, job losses, resource depletion, waste, health risks\n\n**Emergency shelters:** Quick to erect, waterproof, sturdy, portable, affordable, safe"),
  q(7, 'A single fixed pulley has a mechanical advantage of:',
    ['1', '2', '3', '0'], 0,
    'A single fixed pulley has MA = 1 — it does not multiply force. Its only advantage is changing the direction of the pull (you pull down to lift up), which is more convenient.'),
  t(8, "### Key Formulas and Definitions\n\n| Formula / Concept | Description |\n|-------------------|------------|\n| MA = Load ÷ Effort | Mechanical advantage of any machine |\n| MA of pulley system = number of rope sections supporting load | Pulley mechanical advantage |\n| Trade-off | Gain force → lose distance; gain speed → lose force |\n\n### Final Checklist — Can You Do These?\n\n- Follow the IDMEC design process from start to finish?\n- Identify and classify levers (class 1, 2, 3)?\n- Explain the difference between hydraulic and pneumatic systems?\n- Name and describe the five forces (compression, tension, bending, torsion, shear)?\n- Explain stability (centre of gravity, base of support)?\n- List methods of strengthening structures (triangulation, corrugation, cross-bracing)?\n- Draw a circuit diagram with correct symbols?\n- Make and test an electromagnet?\n- Explain how pulleys and cranks work?\n- Discuss the positive and negative impacts of technology?\n- Give examples of indigenous technology in South Africa?\n- Design an emergency meal and shelter?\n- Draw in oblique projection with correct line types?\n- Write a design brief with specifications and constraints?\n\n### Exam Tip\n\nIn the Technology exam, you will need to:\n1. **Sketch** — draw neatly, label everything\n2. **Calculate** — MA and pulley problems\n3. **Explain** — use full sentences, give reasons\n4. **Evaluate** — discuss advantages AND disadvantages\n5. **Apply** — use your knowledge to solve a new problem"),
  fb(9, 'IDMEC stands for Investigate, Design, ___, Evaluate, and Communicate. The strongest shape used in structures is the ___.',
    ['Make', 'triangle'],
    'IDMEC: Investigate, Design, Make, Evaluate, Communicate. The triangle is the strongest shape because it cannot change shape without breaking one of its members — this is why triangulation strengthens structures.'),
  q(10, 'Which of the following best describes the difference between a specification and a constraint?',
    ['A specification is what the product must do; a constraint is a limitation you must work within', 'A specification is a limit; a constraint is a requirement', 'They are the same thing', 'Specifications are only about size'], 0,
    'Specifications define what the product must achieve (must hold 5 kg, must be waterproof). Constraints are the limitations on your design (budget, time, available materials, maximum size).'),
  t(11, "### Safety Review\n\n| Topic | Key Safety Rules |\n|-------|----------------|\n| **Workshop** | Wear goggles, clamp work, cut away from body, keep area tidy |\n| **Electricity** | Only use batteries (low voltage), never create short circuits, check connections first |\n| **Food** | Wash hands, cook thoroughly, keep surfaces clean, check expiry dates |\n| **Materials** | Use tools correctly, wear protective equipment, handle sharp edges carefully |\n\n### South African Context Review\n\n- **Structures:** Eskom pylons (triangulated frame), FNB Stadium (shell), Gariep Dam (solid)\n- **Electricity:** 230 V AC mains, Eskom, coal power, Koeberg nuclear, growing solar/wind\n- **Indigenous:** Iron smelting (200 AD), rondavels, clay grain storage, beadwork\n- **Food:** Fortified maize meal and bread, samp and beans, pap\n- **Emergency:** Gift of the Givers, 2022 KZN floods, disaster relief\n- **Materials:** Corrugated iron ('sinki'), galvanised steel, reinforced concrete\n- **Recycling:** Scrap metal industry, copper wire theft, ferrous vs non-ferrous metals"),
  q(12, 'A block and tackle system has 3 rope sections supporting a 150 N load. What effort is needed?',
    ['50 N', '150 N', '450 N', '3 N'], 0,
    'MA = number of rope sections = 3. Effort = Load ÷ MA = 150 ÷ 3 = 50 N. You pull with 50 N but must pull 3 times as much rope as the load moves.'),
];

